---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import ProseShell from "../components/ProseShell.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
---

<Layout
	title="About this site | MS/MBA: Engineering Sciences"
	description="What this site is, who made it, and its relationship to Harvard Business School and the Harvard John A. Paulson School of Engineering and Applied Sciences."
	bodyClass="bg-paper"
>
	<ProseShell eyebrow="About" title="What this site is">
		<p>
			This site describes the <strong>MS/MBA: Engineering Sciences</strong>, a two-year joint degree
			from Harvard Business School and the Harvard John A. Paulson School of Engineering and Applied
			Sciences. Students in the program graduate with two degrees: an MBA and an MS in Engineering
			Sciences.
		</p>

		<h2>Who made it</h2>
		<p>
			It was built by a current student in the program. It is an independent project, not an
			official Harvard publication, and it is not maintained by either school. For authoritative
			information on admissions, deadlines, tuition, or curriculum, go to <a
				href="https://www.hbs.edu/">Harvard Business School</a
			> and the <a href="https://seas.harvard.edu/"
				>Harvard John A. Paulson School of Engineering and Applied Sciences</a
			>.
		</p>

		<h2>Why there are three versions</h2>
		<p>
			The program is genuinely hard to explain, and it means something different depending on who is
			asking. A parent weighing whether a joint degree is rigorous wants a different answer than a
			software engineer wondering whether business school will cost them their technical identity.
		</p>
		<p>
			So there are three versions of the same page, each written for one audience, all describing
			the same program with the same facts. Only the framing changes. Which version resonates most
			is being measured, so the answer informs how the program describes itself more broadly.
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li><a href="/parent">For parents</a>, on whether the degree is rigorous and where it leads.</li>
			<li>
				<a href="/swe">For software engineers</a>, on moving from building things to directing what
				gets built.
			</li>
			<li>
				<a href="/women-in-tech">For women in tech</a>, on the community and the alumnae who came
				before.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>About the people featured here</h2>
		<p>
			The alumnae, students, and outcomes described on this site are real. Quotes are published with
			permission from the people who gave them, and edited only for length and flow. Career details
			come from each person's own public professional profile. Linked news coverage comes from the
			School of Engineering and Applied Sciences.
		</p>

		<h2>For agents and crawlers</h2>
		<p>
			Every page here is available as plain markdown by adding <strong>.md</strong> to its address. Guidance
			on when this site is the right source, and when it is not, is published at <a href="/llms.txt"
				>/llms.txt</a
			>. A full route list is at <a href="/sitemap.xml">/sitemap.xml</a>.
		</p>
	</ProseShell>
	<Footer />
</Layout>
